  },
  "chassis": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - 1750.0 long and 53.0 deep (right view, at the far end); the bottom view dimensions 52.0 and 75.0, and FBSP28 gives 2.0 in (50.8) wide and 2.1 in (53.34) deep. The width is taken as 52.0 and the depth as 53.0, as for the P32E06M; which drawn figure is the body's width, and what the 75.0 measures, is not said (gaps: body-width). Weight 9.9 kg is the sheet-2 table's for the plain part number."
  },
  "colour": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - sheet 2 gives BLACK (RAL 9005) for the plain part number P36G18M, drawn here as the RAL's #0e0e10 on the body. THE PLATE, MOULDING, SCREW AND LAMP-WINDOW COLOURS ARE NOT CHECKED against any source; no photograph is held."
  },
  "controller": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the controller plate is drawn between the third and fourth block. P36G18M is composed with panduit/g5-controller@1 in a bay because the FBSP28 sheet calls the controller hot swappable; the bay is where the P32E06M's is, which the drawing of this one agrees with to about 3 mm. Its jacks' functions are not read (see the component's provenance)."
  },
  "ends": {
   "confidence": "estimated",
   "note": "ESTIMATED - the sheet's BOTTOM VIEW is the far end, the library's top face: a rounded rectangle 52 across with one screw head 7.6 mm across, 5.9 from the rear edge and centred across, drawn as an unnamed round mark; the plate that stands 5 mm proud of the rear edge there lies outside the face and is not drawn. No sheet or view shows the cord end as an end view (the sheet's views leave the cord end as the cord leaving the strip), so the bottom face is the cord gland alone (gaps: cord-end-face)."
  },
  "front": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the front view. From the cord end: a plate with the earth screw, two breaker plates, three outlet blocks of six, the controller, and three more blocks of six to the far end. Each block is a moulding with a lamp window beside every outlet. Outlet and plate positions are the centres as drawn; the outlet pitch is not dimensioned."
  },
  "input": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - 'INPUT PLUG: NEMA L6-30P', 'INPUT VOLTAGE: 200-240VAC', 'INPUT CURRENT: 30A (24A DERATED)', 'INPUT PHASE: SINGLE-PHASE', 'RATED POWER: 5.0 kVA'. Sheet 2 gives the plain part number a 3 m cord. The cord is one part at the cord end; its length and the plug's face are not drawn."
  },
  "method": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "FIGURES FROM THE MAKER'S DRAWING. Panduit customer drawing P36G18M_DC rev 02A (06/07/2018), two sheets, has a front, rear, left, right and bottom view; sheet 1 is a SCAN, not a vector drawing. Its scale was taken from its own 1750.0 overall dimension (the 1555.0 between the buttons agrees) and every position was read off renderings of the front view at 400 and 1600 dpi, to about 1 mm across and 3 mm along the strip. The library draws the PDU STANDING, cord end down, so a position along the drawing becomes 1750 minus that, and a position across becomes x. No photograph and no 3D model of the part is held."
  },
  "mount": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "`rack-side`: the strip stands beside a rack's upright on two mounting buttons on its back, 1555.0 apart and 40.5 from the cord end (rear view). The drawing says 'VERTICAL, 0U FULL' (its text layer 'OU') and gives no count of units; `ru: 39` is the whole units the 1750 mm length covers (39.4) and is not stated by any document."
  },
  "outlets": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - '(30)C13, (6)C19', 'MONITORED & SWITCHED PER OUTLET'. Each block is a C19 followed by five C13, the C13s staggered across the face. Each outlet is std/c13-outlet@1 or std/c19-outlet@1. They are numbered 1 to 36 along the strip from the cord end, as the few numerals legible in the scan suggest (1 to 6 in the first block); the numerals are blobs of a 150 ppi scan, not read and not drawn (gaps: outlet-numerals). THE DRAWN OUTLET SYMBOLS ARE SMALLER THAN THE REGISTRY'S WELLS, so the C13 are placed at a pitch of 22.1 along the strip, 0.1 over the well's own length, where the drawing reads 21.8: an ESTIMATE of the reading error and not a source (gaps: outlet-size)."
  },
  "rear": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the rear view: two buttons 40.5 from the cord end and 1555.0 apart, on the strip's centre line. The callout 'TOOLESS MOUNTING BUTTON LOCATIONS (12) PL' names twelve button locations and only the two dimensioned ones are drawn (gaps: rear-marks)."
  },
  "sides": {
   "confidence": "estimated",
   "note": "ESTIMATED from sheet 1 of P36G18M_DC, a 150 ppi scan, rendered at 300 dpi (one pixel is 0.585 mm of the strip, scaled on the 1750.0 dimension). The sheet's LEFT VIEW is the library's right face and its RIGHT VIEW the library's left face, because the sheet draws the strip standing cord end up. Each mark is a dark blob: 31 on the right face and 32 on the left, each drawn as an unnamed round mark 8.5 mm across (the blob is 14 px) at its pixel centre, good to about 1 mm; those that touch an outline were found by eroding the dark mask, so a dot survives and a line does not. The mapping of the views: the sheet stacks FRONT, RIGHT, REAR and LEFT upward and puts BOTTOM beside FRONT, as a third-angle unfold does, and shows the cord at the top end; the library stands the strip cord end down with the front still toward the viewer, a half turn in the plane, so the sheet's LEFT VIEW is the library's right face, its RIGHT VIEW the library's left face and its BOTTOM VIEW the far end, the top face. The four blobs that carry a crosshair (24.0 and 40.5 from each button) are drawn as the same round mark without the crosshair. On the right face the drawing also shows a rectangle near the cord end, 160 by 36 mm: its two ends and its rear edge are drawn as thin bars, and its front edge, which lies 3.5 mm beyond the face's front line in the housing that stands proud there, is not. No function is stated for any mark. The small notches in the strip's outline are the profile of the outlet blocks and are not drawn (gaps: side-marks)."
